{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Design Draft and Theory-to-Prototype Alignment", "title": "Problem Statement and Learner Profile", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting problem statement and learner profile to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment", "ail606_supplement_problem_statement_and_learner_profile", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 1, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "04", "section": "01", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/03/08"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_and_learner_profile_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_and_learner_profile_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_and_learner_profile_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_and_learner_profile_calibration"]}, "content": "# Problem Statement and Learner Profile\n\n![Problem Statement and Learner Profile textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/04-01-problem-statement-and-learner-profile.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nEvery instructional design project begins with two claims that the rest of the design has to honor: a claim about the gap (the problem statement) and a claim about who has the gap (the learner profile). When these two claims are vague, every downstream decision becomes arbitrary, because there is nothing concrete to design against. AIL 606 treats the problem statement and learner profile as the load-bearing foundation of the storyboard: each frame you later draft should be defensible by pointing back to a specific performance gap and a specific learner attribute.\n\nThis section teaches you to write a problem statement that locates a performance gap (not a feeling) and a learner profile that drives design (not flatters the audience). You should leave able to test whether a profile attribute actually constrains a frame decision, and able to explain why \"make it more engaging\" is not yet a designable goal.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write a problem statement that names a located performance gap, its conditions, and a measurable target.\n- Build a learner profile in which every attribute changes at least one storyboard decision.\n- Separate design-driving attributes (prior knowledge, context of use, language) from flattering adjectives (motivated, smart).\n- Document how a revised problem statement changes which evidence would count as success.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA district hires you to fix a safety onboarding module because \"completion rates are low.\" You could accept that framing and start redesigning screens. Instead, you pull the analytics and find that 70 percent of new hires reach screen 2 and then abandon, almost all within the first three days of hire. Screen 2 opens with nine unexplained acronyms. The \"low completion\" symptom is now a located gap: novices with low domain vocabulary hit a recall wall before any practice. The redesign target writes itself, and it has nothing to do with engagement.\n\nThe lesson of this case is that the presenting complaint and the actual problem are rarely the same sentence. The problem statement is where you do the work of converting a complaint into something a storyboard can be built against.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA problem statement is the gap between current and desired performance, stated with enough specificity that you could later test whether it closed. A useful problem statement carries four parts: the actor (who is failing to perform), the located gap (where in the task or screen the performance breaks down), the conditions (the context and constraints under which it happens), and a measurable target (what \"fixed\" would look like in numbers or observable behavior). Strip any of these and the statement degrades into a slogan.\n\nThe learner profile is the discipline of describing your audience only in terms that change a design decision. The relevant categories come straight from instructional analysis: prior knowledge (what schemas the learner already holds), context of use (device, time pressure, environment), and learner variability (language, accessibility needs, motivation barriers). The test for any profile line is the design-consequence test: if changing the attribute would change no frame, no media choice, and no sequence, it does not belong in the profile.\n\n| Weak attribute (no design consequence) | Design-driving attribute | What it constrains |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| \"Motivated and eager to learn\" | \"Completes the module in 5-minute bursts between shifts\" | Resumable checkpoints; short segments |\n| \"Tech-savvy adults\" | \"First exposure to compliance vocabulary\" | Glossary tooltip before the acronym list |\n| \"Diverse learners\" | \"Roughly 1 in 5 use a screen reader\" | Alt text and reading-order pass on every frame |\n\nThe deeper principle is that the problem statement and profile must be mutually consistent. A target of \"under 90 seconds to first correct action\" is only meaningful given the profile that says the user is a novice working on a shared tablet between shifts. The number and the person define each other.\n\nRun the design-consequence test yourself: match each design-driving profile attribute to the single storyboard constraint it forces. Each pairing is the proof that the attribute earns its place.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Completes the module in 5-minute bursts between shifts\",\"definition\":\"Resumable checkpoints and short, self-contained segments\"},{\"term\":\"First exposure to compliance vocabulary\",\"definition\":\"Glossary tooltip placed before the acronym list\"},{\"term\":\"Roughly 1 in 5 learners use a screen reader\",\"definition\":\"Alt text and a reading-order pass on every frame\"},{\"term\":\"Works on a shared shop-floor tablet, not a personal device\",\"definition\":\"No saved login state; no reliance on prior personal history\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A draft profile line calls the learners intelligent adults who care about safety. Why does this line not belong in a design-driving profile?\" explanation=\"The line is praise that changes no frame, media choice, or sequence. A profile attribute earns its place only when changing it would change a design decision; intelligence and caring constrain nothing in the storyboard.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It changes no storyboard frame, media choice, or sequence, so it is flattery, not analysis.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is too short to count as a full attribute.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It should be moved into the problem statement instead.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is accurate, so it is fine to keep for morale.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWork through one annotated before/after revision of a problem statement and learner profile, the way you would defend it in a design review.\n\n**Before (draft packet, cover panel):**\n\n> Problem: Learners are not engaging with the safety onboarding module. Audience: adult learners who are motivated and tech-savvy. Goal: make the module more engaging.\n\n**Annotations a reviewer would leave:**\n\n1. \"Not engaging\" is a symptom, not a located gap. Where exactly does the learner stall, and what creates the demand there?\n2. \"Motivated and tech-savvy\" are praise adjectives. Neither one would change a single storyboard frame, so they do not belong in a design-driving profile.\n3. \"Make it more engaging\" is a solution dressed as a goal. There is nothing here a later revision could be tested against.\n\n**After (revised cover panel):**\n\n> Problem: New hires in days 0 to 30, with low domain vocabulary, abandon the onboarding module at screen 2, where an unexplained 9-acronym list imposes a high recall load before any practice.\n> Learner profile: low prior domain knowledge; completing the module on a shared shop-floor tablet between shifts (short, interrupted sessions); first exposure to the compliance vocabulary.\n> Goal: reduce time-to-first-correct-action for novices from 4 minutes to under 90 seconds.\n\n**Why the after version is stronger:** each profile attribute now constrains a frame decision (the interrupted-session attribute justifies resumable checkpoints; the low-vocabulary attribute justifies a glossary tooltip before the acronym list), the gap is located at screen 2 with a named source of demand, and the goal is measurable, so the next storyboard revision can be evaluated against it. The change is a better evidence trace, not longer wording.\n\nWalk the revision the way a reviewer would, one judgment at a time. Predict what is wrong before each reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Revising the cover panel, one judgment at a time\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The draft goal is to make the module more engaging. What is the core defect of this as a goal?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It is a solution dressed as a goal, with nothing a later revision could be tested against\",\"It is too ambitious for a single module\",\"It names the wrong audience\",\"It uses informal language\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Make it more engaging prescribes a fix instead of naming a measurable end state. There is no number or observable behavior a revision could be checked against, so it fails the testability check.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The analytics show 70 percent of new hires stall at screen 2 within their first three days. What does this convert the complaint into?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A located gap: novices with low vocabulary hit a recall wall at screen 2\",\"Proof the module is too long\",\"Evidence that learners are unmotivated\",\"A reason to add gamification\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"The data locates the gap at a specific screen, for a specific actor, under specific conditions. The symptom of low completion is now an analyzable performance gap with a named source of demand.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Final form: novices in days 0 to 30, with low domain vocabulary, abandon at screen 2 where an unexplained 9-acronym list imposes high recall load; reduce time-to-first-correct-action from 4 minutes to under 90 seconds. Actor, located gap, conditions, and a measurable target now tell one coherent story.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTreat each profile attribute as a hypothesis about design, then keep only the ones that pay for themselves in a decision. Apply three tests before the storyboard frame sequence leaves the cover panel:\n\n1. **Located-gap check:** Does the problem statement name where performance breaks, under what conditions, for whom?\n2. **Design-consequence check:** Does every profile line change at least one frame, media choice, or sequence? Delete the rest.\n3. **Testability check:** Is the target stated so that you could later measure whether it closed?\n\nIf you cannot answer all three, the cover panel is written but not yet designable.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is writing the learner profile as a compliment to the audience. Adjectives like \"motivated\" and \"intelligent\" feel respectful but constrain nothing; they are decoration, not analysis. The second error is treating the presenting complaint as the problem statement, which freezes the design around a symptom and quietly skips the analysis that would have relocated the gap.\n\nA profile is design analysis, not audience flattery, and a problem statement is a located gap, not a restated complaint.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nBegin with the complaint exactly as the client gave it, then refuse to design against it until you have located the gap. Pull whatever evidence exists (analytics, observation, a brief task analysis) and find the screen, step, or moment where performance actually breaks. Convert that into the four-part statement: actor, located gap, conditions, measurable target.\n\nNow build the profile by reverse-engineering it from the gap. Ask what about these learners makes this particular break happen here. Low vocabulary explains the acronym wall; interrupted sessions explain abandonment mid-module. Each attribute you add should answer a \"why does the gap occur\" question, which guarantees it has a design consequence.\n\nFinally, stress-test consistency. Read the target number aloud and ask whether it makes sense for the person in the profile. If \"under 90 seconds\" assumes an expert but the profile says novice, one of the two is wrong. The cover panel is finished when the gap, the person, and the number tell one coherent story.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence** on the right. The claim is the design decision you think a profile attribute is forcing; the evidence is the specific frame, sequence, or media choice it constrains. By the end of AIL 606 04.01, your notes should let a reviewer reconstruct why each profile line earns its place.\n\nA strong reader of a problem statement and learner profile can answer four questions:\n\n1. What located gap does this statement name, and how would you measure it?\n2. Which single profile attribute is doing the most design work here?\n3. Which profile line could be deleted with no effect on the storyboard?\n4. How would the target change if the device or prior knowledge changed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reviewer can trace a straight line from a profile attribute to a frame decision to the measurable target. Treat polish as the last layer; the foundation is the located gap, the design-driving profile, and the testable goal. Where an attribute does not yet connect to a frame, mark it with the annotation tools and ask BigAL for a targeted comparison between a flattering profile and a design-driving one rather than a generic explanation.\n\nA useful final paragraph for the artifact would sound like this: \"I rewrote the problem statement to locate the gap at screen 2 and set a 90-second target, and I kept only the three profile attributes that each force a specific frame change. I dropped 'motivated and tech-savvy' because it changed nothing in the storyboard.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same module from new-hire onboarding to annual refresher training for experienced staff. The presenting complaint (\"low completion\") may look identical, but the profile inverts: prior knowledge is now high, so the acronym wall is no longer the gap, and a 90-second novice target is meaningless. The located gap might shift to \"experienced staff skim and miss the one policy change this year.\" Transfer here means re-running the analysis, not copying the cover panel; if you understand the section, the new profile produces a different problem statement.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/04-01-problem-statement-and-learner-profile-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"04.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 04.01. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_problem_statement_and_learner_profile\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_problem_statement_and_learner_profile\", \"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links the located gap, the design-driving profile attributes, and the measurable target into one consistent story. That coherence is what separates instructional analysis from a restated complaint.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_and_learner_profile_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_problem_statement_and_learner_profile", "stem": "A storyboard packet opens with the problem statement: \"Learners do not engage with the onboarding module.\" Which revision makes this a designer-grade problem statement rather than a complaint?", "options": ["\"Learners do not engage with the onboarding module, so we should make it more interesting.\"", "\"New hires (0 to 30 days, low domain vocabulary) abandon the onboarding module after screen 2, where an unexplained acronym list creates a 9-item recall demand before any practice.\"", "\"The onboarding module needs a refresh because engagement is low.\"", "\"Stakeholders feel the onboarding module is outdated and want it modernized.\""], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A defensible problem statement names the specific learner segment, the observable failure point, and the source of demand. The other options state a symptom or a solution without an evidenced learner profile.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_solution_first", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_problem_statement_and_learner_profile"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_and_learner_profile_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_problem_statement_and_learner_profile", "stem": "Your learner profile says \"adult learners who are motivated and tech-savvy.\" Why will a reviewer flag this as insufficient for storyboard decisions?", "options": ["It is too short and should be at least one paragraph.", "It uses flattering adjectives instead of design-relevant variability such as prior knowledge, access conditions, or task context that would change a frame decision.", "It does not name the instructor.", "It should list the learners by name."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A learner profile earns its place only when each attribute could change a design choice. Generic praise like motivated and tech-savvy does not constrain any storyboard frame.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_generic_persona", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_problem_statement_and_learner_profile"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_and_learner_profile_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "stem": "You ask an AI tool to draft a learner profile for your storyboard. What is the strongest bounded use of that output?", "options": ["Paste the AI persona directly into the packet because it reads smoothly.", "Treat the AI persona as a hypothesis, then keep only the attributes you can confirm against your intake survey or context evidence and reject the invented ones.", "Avoid AI because learner profiles must be fully original.", "Ask the AI to also pick the storyboard frames so the profile and frames stay consistent."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "AI can propose candidate attributes, but the designer must verify each against real evidence and discard fabricated demographics. Accepting it whole or letting it drive frame choices removes designer judgment.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_ai_persona_as_fact", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_and_learner_profile_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "Two problem statements target the same module. Statement A: \"Improve the module.\" Statement B: \"Reduce the time-to-first-correct-action for novice users from 4 minutes to under 90 seconds.\" Why is B the stronger design anchor?", "options": ["B is longer and uses more numbers.", "B states a measurable target tied to a specific learner action, so a later storyboard revision can be tested against it.", "B avoids naming the learner, which keeps it flexible.", "B sounds more professional."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A bounded, measurable target lets you decide later whether a revision actually moved the design outcome. Vague improvement claims cannot be evaluated.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_unmeasurable_goal", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "98cb469b42bba6c34068a7ba911943f0fe3a7c7ec813d22156744db232150944", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_solution_first", "pattern": "solution_stated_as_problem", "description": "Writing a problem statement that names a desired solution or a vague symptom instead of the evidenced gap and its source of demand", "trigger": "problem statement says make it interesting or needs a refresh rather than locating the observable failure point", "feedback": "State the learner segment, the observable failure point, and the source of demand before any solution. The fix is not the problem.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_generic_persona", "pattern": "flattering_generic_persona", "description": "Building a learner profile from praise adjectives that do not constrain any design decision", "trigger": "profile lists motivated, tech-savvy, or eager without design-relevant variability", "feedback": "Keep only attributes that would change a frame: prior knowledge, access conditions, task context. Drop adjectives that change nothing.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_ai_persona_as_fact", "pattern": "ai_persona_treated_as_evidence", "description": "Accepting an AI-generated persona as verified learner data instead of a hypothesis to confirm", "trigger": "learner pastes AI demographics into the packet without checking intake or context evidence", "feedback": "Treat AI personas as candidate attributes. Confirm each against real intake or context data and reject fabricated ones.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_1_problem_statement_unmeasurable_goal", "pattern": "unmeasurable_design_goal", "description": "Setting a problem goal that cannot be tested by a later revision", "trigger": "goal says improve or enhance with no measurable learner action or target", "feedback": "Tie the goal to a measurable learner action so a later storyboard revision can be evaluated against it.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}